  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,|4n|\\ge 8}$\n- $B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,|4q|\\ge 8}$\n- $C={x\\in R\\,:\\,|4x|\\ge 8}$",
  "worked_solution": "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nWe start by solving the following inequality for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|4x|\\ge 8 \\\\ 4|x|\\ge 8 \\\\ |x|\\ge 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\nx\\le -2\\,\\text{or}\\,x\\ge 2\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in I=(-\\infty ,-2]\\cup [2,\\infty ).$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. If $n\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality $|4n|\\ge 8$ are all the integers that lie in the interval $I.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nA={\u2026,-3,-2}\\cup {2,3,\u2026}.\n$$\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. If $q\\in Q,$ the solutions to $|4q|\\ge 8$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval $I.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B\u2282C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A,B,$ and $C.$\"",
